\section{Bayesian optimization and compute}
The demonstration fits a single-task Gaussian process to twelve noisy synthetic
observations and uses log expected improvement to select one point on a discrete
normalized grid. The objective is an illustrative smooth surface, not a CFPS
surrogate. Actual selection requires a quantized feasible candidate set,
experimental replicate noise, batch effects, and controls.

BoTorch runs on Apple Silicon using CPU double precision. This project explicitly
uses \texttt{torch.double} and \texttt{device="cpu"}. PyTorch's MPS backend does
not support float64, while BoTorch recommends double precision for Gaussian-process
stability\cite{botorch,mps}. Modal supplies remote Linux CPU execution of the same
package and lockfile. From the project root:
\begin{Verbatim}
uv run --extra cloud modal setup
uv run --extra cloud modal run modal_app.py
uv run --extra cloud modal run modal_app.py \
  --construct constructs/competition/sfgfp.json
\end{Verbatim}
The runner requests two CPU cores, 4 GiB RAM, and a ten-minute timeout. It returns
the model trajectory, SBML, and synthetic BO result into \texttt{results/modal}.
Compute is billed to the authenticated Modal account. Independent ensemble
members and simulated campaigns are natural future parallel workloads.

\section{Computational experiments and a worked calibration study}
The command \texttt{cfps-sim experiments} runs ten reference/process/resource
conditions, six local kinetic sensitivities, a sixteen-condition ATP/GTP grid,
a twenty-five-condition transcription/translation grid, two assumed transcript
boundaries, and sixty-four perturbed scenarios. It checks finite outputs,
nonnegative trajectories, individual amino-acid balances, required-resource
controls, and agreement with a tighter solver tolerance. Parameter and initial
pool overrides are explicitly validated; unsupported chemistry is rejected.
These interventions occur in model space and do not constitute feasible
competition recipes. Removing fuel need not eliminate protein synthesis because
initial nucleotide pools remain. Scenario quantiles use assumed independent
lognormal perturbations, not calibrated biological uncertainty.

\begin{Verbatim}
uv run cfps-sim experiments --output results/experiments --seed 2026
uv run cfps-sim calibrate results/experiments/synthetic/study.json \
  results/experiments/synthetic/observations.csv --output results/refit
\end{Verbatim}

\subsection{Published example: dynamic CFPS calibration}
Horvath et al. provide a relevant experimental example\cite{horvath}. Their
glucose-powered CAT model fits protein and metabolite time courses, uses
literature-bounded parameter ensembles, and challenges metabolic reaction groups.
Their author repository contains raw measurements and fitted/random ensembles
\cite{horvath_code}. We would first reproduce that original model and data before
adapting mechanisms to the competition. The lesson for our simpler model is to
align observables and units, constrain effective parameters, examine multiple
compatible fits, and use interventions to discriminate explanations. The
CAT system is not our sfGFP competition dataset; transferring its numerical
parameters requires checking model definitions and experimental conditions.

\subsection{Runnable example: recovery from explicitly synthetic observations}
The implemented example generates GFP and mRNA concentrations at eight times
in three training conditions and one withheld condition, with two replicates
and independent Gaussian noise. Known channel SD is 5\% of the maximum
concentration for that condition. Two effective parameters are fitted:
translation capacity $k_{\mathrm{tl}}$ and machinery decay $k_{\mathrm{decay}}$.
All other chemistry, transcript bounds, pools, and observation scaling are fixed.
Bounded least squares minimizes training residuals divided by their supplied SD
in log-parameter space. Two initializations provide a basic optimizer check.
The fitter never reads generating parameter values or withheld measurements
when evaluating the training objective. Tests deliberately alter withheld
measurements to check this separation.

<<computational_study, echo=False, results='tex'>>=
from cfps_sim.experiments import run_suite, make_synthetic_study, calibrate
import numpy as np
study_output = Path("../results/article-experiments")
challenge_report = run_suite("../constructs/competition/sfgfp.json", study_output, seed=2026)
study_path = make_synthetic_study("../constructs/competition/sfgfp.json", study_output / "synthetic", seed=2026)
fit_report = calibrate(study_path, study_path.parent / "observations.csv", study_output / "calibration")
truth = json.loads(study_path.read_text())["synthetic_truth"]
print(r"\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{lrr}Parameter & Generating value & Fitted value\\\hline")
for name in truth:
    print(name.replace("_", r"\_") + f" & {truth[name]:.5g} & {fit_report['fitted_parameters'][name]:.5g}" + r"\\")
print(r"\end{tabular}\end{center}")
metric = next(row for row in fit_report["metrics"] if row["split"] == "test" and row["observable"] == "GFP")
print(f"All five computational checks pass: {challenge_report['all_checks_pass']}. "
      f"Withheld GFP RMSE is {1000*metric['rmse_mM']:.4f} micromolar after fitting, "
      f"compared with {1000*metric['baseline_rmse_mM']:.4f} micromolar for default parameters.")
print(r"\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{lr}Computational result & Value\\\hline")
print(f"Maximum amino-acid balance error (mM) & {challenge_report['maximum_aa_balance_error_mM']:.2e}" + r"\\")
print(f"Relative endpoint difference after solver refinement & {challenge_report['solver_relative_endpoint_difference']:.2e}" + r"\\")
print(f"Conditional parameter Jacobian rank & {fit_report['jacobian_rank']} of 2" + r"\\")
print(f"Withheld GFP RMSE reduction versus defaults & {100*(1-metric['rmse_mM']/metric['baseline_rmse_mM']):.1f}" + r"\%\\")
print(r"\end{tabular}\end{center}")
grid = np.genfromtxt(study_output / "atp_gtp_grid.csv", delimiter=",", names=True)
capacity_grid = np.genfromtxt(study_output / "tx_tl_grid.csv", delimiter=",", names=True)
with (study_output / "calibration/predictions.csv").open() as handle:
    import csv
    heldout = [row for row in csv.DictReader(handle) if row["split"] == "test" and row["observable"] == "GFP"]
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(11, 3.2))
im = axes[0].imshow(1000*grid["gfp_mM"].reshape(4, 4), origin="lower", aspect="auto")
axes[0].set(xticks=range(4), xticklabels=[0.25, 0.75, 1.5, 3], yticks=range(4), yticklabels=[0.25, 0.75, 1.5, 3], xlabel="Initial GTP (mM)", ylabel="Initial ATP (mM)")
fig.colorbar(im, ax=axes[0], label="GFP (micromolar)")
im = axes[1].imshow(1000*capacity_grid["gfp_mM"].reshape(5, 5), origin="lower", aspect="auto")
axes[1].set(xticks=range(5), xticklabels=[0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4], yticks=range(5), yticklabels=[0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4], xlabel="Translation scale", ylabel="Transcription scale")
fig.colorbar(im, ax=axes[1], label="GFP (micromolar)")
times = [float(row["time_min"]) for row in heldout]
axes[2].scatter(times, [1000*float(row["value_mM"]) for row in heldout], s=12, label="Synthetic data")
axes[2].plot(times, [1000*float(row["predicted_mM"]) for row in heldout], label="Fit")
axes[2].plot(times, [1000*float(row["baseline_mM"]) for row in heldout], linestyle="--", label="Defaults")
axes[2].set(xlabel="Time (min)", ylabel="GFP (micromolar)")
axes[2].legend(fontsize=7)
for ax, title in zip(axes, ["A. Nucleotide pools", "B. Effective capacities", "C. Withheld condition"]):
    ax.set_title(title, fontsize=10)
fig.tight_layout()
fig.savefig("figures/computational-study.pdf", bbox_inches="tight")
plt.close(fig)
@

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/computational-study.pdf}
\caption{Model-space resource and capacity challenges, followed by prediction
of a withheld synthetic condition. Every panel uses the reduced uncalibrated
model; none represents experimental competition measurements.}
\label{fig:computational-study}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Interpretation of the computational results}
The checks establish that removing a required resource or expression process
blocks the corresponding output, that amino-acid consumption matches sequence
demand, and that tightening solver tolerances leaves the reference endpoint
essentially unchanged. Fuel removal is a distinct intervention: initial NTPs
remain available and can support expression without regeneration. Absence of
mature GFP after disabling maturation also does not imply absence of translated
protein. These controls distinguish model behavior from assay interpretation.

The two response grids in Figure~\ref{fig:computational-study} explore nucleotide
availability and expression-capacity balance within the reduced equations.
Neither grid estimates optimal reagent concentrations or measured T7 activity.
Local sensitivities apply near the reference state, while the sixty-four-draw
ensemble describes spread under declared perturbation assumptions. Transcript
boundary changes alter nucleotide demand even when their endpoint effect is
small under the chosen pools. Experimental transcript lengths and lysate
composition would be needed to decide which scenario is appropriate.

The synthetic fit has a full-rank two-parameter local Jacobian and improves
withheld GFP error relative to defaults. This is conditional identifiability
with fixed pools, kinetics, observation scaling, and known error SD, rather
than evidence that the same two parameters can be resolved from competition
endpoints. Both the generating and fitted models have the same reaction
structure. Missing chemistry therefore cannot be diagnosed by this recovery
exercise; an experimental fit must additionally examine systematic residuals,
independent batches, and performance against an empirical baseline.

The reported Jacobian rank and singular values describe local identifiability
conditional on fixed chemistry. Approximate 95\% parameter intervals use a local
Gaussian approximation with known independent measurement SD. They exclude
model discrepancy, batch effects, uncertain pools, and transcript uncertainty,
and no interval-coverage claim is made. The CSV interface supports GFP, mRNA,
ATP, GTP, CTP, and UTP concentrations in mM, with time in minutes and source
provenance. Raw fluorescence or enzyme activity cannot be passed as protein
concentrations without a validated observation mapping. Conditions and all their
replicates are assigned wholly to training or testing. This same-model recovery
exercise establishes fitting behavior; experimental calibration remains open.

\section{Calibration and prospective experimental comparison}
Lee's study offers an experimental template for this stage: swap functional
modules, localize a failure, intervene, and compare the rebuilt system with
working references. The published work includes replicated expression
comparisons and reports at least three replicates per experiment\cite{chae}.
We propose adapting that logic to competition supplements. It does not supply
a fitted kinetic model of this lysate, and its purified factors cannot establish
our endogenous enzyme activities. Calibration and prospective validation are
therefore new work in the competition system.

\subsection{Separate measurement calibration from biochemical calibration}
First calibrate the observation process. For sfGFP, use the platform's applicable
standard curve, blanks, dilution factors, and linear measurement range; quantify
background and repeated-reference variability. Fluorescence reports mature
fluorescent protein, so changes in maturation or matrix effects can mimic changes
in total synthesis. For PETase and reteplase, preserve the substrate, assay timing,
dilution, and activity units. Their post-expression product-release curves
constrain the assay process rather than the preceding synthesis trajectory.
Where available, an independent protein measurement can help separate abundance
from active fraction. This is a proposed measurement plan, subject to what the
competition platform exposes; missing measurements must remain missing.

Next map verified recipe concentrations into initial model conditions, documenting
base-buffer contributions and unknown lysate pools. This mapping is not yet
implemented. Start with few effective parameters supported by the observations,
such as aggregate expression capacity, energy-regeneration capacity, and
machinery decay. An endpoint alone cannot separately identify transcription,
translation, degradation, and maturation rates. Use available expression time
courses or orthogonal measurements when possible, and otherwise constrain or
fix unidentifiable quantities with explicitly uncertain priors. Profile or
posterior uncertainty should reveal parameter combinations the data cannot
resolve, rather than force a unique biochemical interpretation.

An initial observation model can be written
\[
y_{t,b,r}(x)=h_t\!\left(z(x;\theta_b)\right)+\delta_t(x)
            +\beta_{t,b}+\epsilon_{t,b,r},
\]
where $x$ is a feasible recipe, $z$ is the mechanistic state, $h_t$ maps it to
target $t$'s actual assay, $\theta_b$ contains identifiable batch-dependent
effective parameters, $\delta_t$ models systematic discrepancy, $\beta_{t,b}$
is a batch offset, and $\epsilon_{t,b,r}$ is replicate noise. A GP can supply
the discrepancy term. Batch capacity and offset can themselves be confounded;
fit only the terms justified by repeated controls and independent batches.
For sfGFP, the current mature-protein state is an input to $h_t$, not an already
calibrated fluorescence prediction. Keep experimental data and simulated outputs
separate throughout fitting. This equation describes a planned model, not an
implemented calibration result.

\subsection{Module experiments that discriminate hypotheses}
Begin with two feasible reference recipes and exchange their salt/buffer and
energy/nucleotide modules in a crossed design, holding the remaining additions
and supplied DNA constant. Validate every hybrid against fixed base contributions,
stock semantics, dispensing increments, and volume limits. A coupled change in
salts and nucleotides must be recorded as such. Retain the original references,
no-DNA controls where allowed, and replicated hybrids; randomize positions and
record plate, lysate lot, preparation, incubation, and dispensing metadata.
Use the results to test whether module effects transfer between backgrounds
or require an interaction, rather than assume every successful swap identifies
a single kinetic mechanism. Repeated wells estimate technical variation;
independent preparations or lysate lots are needed to assess reproducibility.
The panel is our adaptation of i-POPFLEX's diagnostic strategy, not a reproduction
of its purified-component experiment.

\subsection{Prospective comparison under an equal experimental budget}
Before model-guided selection, compare a data-only GP, the calibrated mechanistic
model, and a mechanistic-plus-discrepancy model on withheld complete recipes and
batches. Keep all replicates of one recipe together. Report predictive error and
interval coverage in each target's own units. Endpoint predictive accuracy and
the ability to select improvements are separate questions.

For a prospective campaign, start three policies from the same measured seed
panel: a quantized space-filling or random baseline, data-only Bayesian
optimization, and mechanistic-plus-discrepancy Bayesian optimization. Give them
the same candidate constraints, rounds, assay access, and well budget, counting
controls, replicates, and calibration measurements. Use the same information
sharing rule for all policies and disclose it; extra measurements supplied only
to one arm are an unequal budget. Keep policy conditions represented on each
plate when possible so policy and plate effects are not confounded.

Freeze each model, candidate list, and uncertainty interval before the next
batch is measured; retrain only after that batch is returned. Predefine success
as improvement in the best independently confirmed target readout over the
competition reference per experimental budget, with a practical effect threshold
and uncertainty. Also report learning curves and prospective interval coverage.
Retest nominees from every policy together in independent preparations with
common references and blinded identifiers where feasible. This reduces selection
of a noisy apparent winner. If the available competition budget cannot support
three arms, report the narrower comparison and its limits rather than claim a
general advantage for mechanistic optimization. Repeated campaigns are needed
to estimate policy reliability. The present repository establishes execution
and accounting; this proposed comparison requires experimental access and a
calibrated recipe-to-model connection.

\input{research-context.tex}

\section{Validation and next milestones}
The starter checks cover recipe arithmetic, dispensing increments, finite and
nonnegative trajectories within tolerance, an amino-acid/protein balance, and
absence of expression when nucleotide substrates are absent. Construct checks
verify all three source protein matches, lossless GenBank exchange, refusal of
modified checksums and implicit transcript boundaries, individual amino-acid
conservation, absence of translation when a required amino acid is depleted,
and replay of exported sequence-aware SBML. Passing these checks
establishes software behavior, not empirical predictive validity.

During initial verification, both core checks passed on Apple Silicon. The
synthetic BoTorch demo ran locally and on a Modal Linux CPU using float64;
both selected the normalized grid point $(0.60,0.35)$. The remote run also
completed the RoadRunner simulation. These smoke runs verify the execution
paths and are not tests of biological prediction or optimization performance.
The expanded suite now passes nine tests, including synthetic recovery and
withheld-data isolation. A further Modal run used the supplied
sfGFP artifact and all twenty amino-acid pools; its final mature-protein
concentration agreed with the local trajectory within $2\times10^{-18}$ mM.
This agreement checks cross-platform execution, not biological accuracy.
The Linux RoadRunner wheel requires the shared Python 3.11 library. GitHub CI
therefore provisions Python with \texttt{actions/setup-python} and explicitly
selects it during \texttt{uv sync}; the initial standalone uv interpreter failed
to load \texttt{libpython3.11.so.1.0}. Modal's system interpreter supplies this
library. A Linux installation must make its shared Python library available to
the dynamic loader before importing RoadRunner.

Repository instructions require updating this Pweave source and rebuilding its
tracked PDF when scientific behavior, inputs, assumptions, workflows, or
validation change. The article remains synchronized with the implemented project.

Next, audit well-level observations and metadata; reconcile reference recipes;
map recipes to initial conditions; refine assumed transcript boundaries; source
parameters with uncertainty ranges; and fit a small identifiable parameter set.
Evaluate whole held-out recipes and batches against an empirical baseline before
using mechanistic predictions in experiment selection. Add PETase and reteplase
with separate folding and assay observation models. Assay product time courses
must not be treated as expression time courses.

\section{Conclusions and future work}
CFPS-Sim now provides a reproducible path from audited inputs to resource-model
simulation, software challenges, and conditional parameter fitting. Three
supplied constructs retain sequence provenance and source-protein checks;
sfGFP simulations account for twenty amino-acid pools and assumed transcript
demand. Nine software tests and Linux CI pass. The computational suite passes
five numerical/structural checks, and the synthetic calibration recovers both
generating rates within 3\%, reducing withheld GFP RMSE by approximately 31\%
relative to default parameters. These results support the execution and fitting
workflow. They do not establish that the model predicts experimental yield,
that its uncertainty intervals are calibrated, or that mechanistic selection
outperforms data-only Bayesian optimization.

The main scientific gap is the connection from feasible recipes and uncertain
lysate composition to measured target performance. An audited concentration
mapping and observation calibration should precede additional kinetic detail.
We propose a small effective-parameter model constrained by expression time
courses where available, followed by tests on complete withheld recipes and
independent lysate batches. Magnesium/speciation and nucleotide regeneration
are early mechanistic priorities. Separate folding and activity models are
needed before extending sfGFP findings to PETase and reteplase.

\subsection{Competition track: adapt POSTECH's diagnostic module exchanges}
Joongoo Lee's group demonstrates a useful experimental principle: exchange
functional groups between working and problematic preparations, localize a
failure, change the preparation, and confirm recovered function\cite{chae,chae_preprint}.
Our first proposed competition study applies that principle to supplements.
Select two feasible reference backgrounds, A and B, with identical non-exchanged
additions, or construct and label matched derivatives of the references.
Cross their salt/buffer and energy/nucleotide modules while holding the
remaining additions and supplied DNA constant. The four conditions are
$S_AE_A$, $S_AE_B$, $S_BE_A$, and $S_BE_B$; the two matched parental combinations
remain references within the panel. Every
combination must satisfy fixed-base, stock, transfer, and volume constraints.
If those constraints prevent independent module exchange, redefine the panel
and report the coupled changes rather than label it an independent factorial.

A first plate could use three technical replicates per condition, giving twelve
condition wells, plus applicable blanks or no-DNA controls where the platform
permits them. If matched derivatives replace actual competition references,
include those references separately and count their wells in the budget.
Randomize well positions and record lysate lot, incubation,
dispensing, and assay metadata. Analyze the two module effects and their
interaction using the actual target readout; do not infer individual enzyme
activities from module effects alone. Repeat the panel with an independent
preparation or lot before claiming transferability. This initial panel tests
module behavior and technical variation, not optimization-policy superiority.

Use the panel and applicable time courses to compare empirical prediction with
a minimally calibrated mechanistic model. Freeze predictions before a new batch,
retain repeated references, and confirm candidates independently. Only then
start the equal-budget selection-policy comparison described above. Extend
successful modules to PETase and reteplase as separate target studies with
their own activity observations. A reproducible negative result is informative:
it can reject a transferable-module hypothesis or motivate an explicit
interaction or folding model.

\subsection{Reconstitution track: reproduce and model the i-POPFLEX workflow}
A separate laboratory study could reproduce the published i-POPFLEX preparation
using its component definitions, DNA templates, purification protocol, and
OT-2 workflow\cite{chae}. The study assembles in-vitro-produced translation
factors and split T7 polymerase with ribosomes, tRNA, and buffer; it is distinct
from supplementation of the competition's fixed lysate. Begin by auditing the
available supplementary protocols and reagent access, preserving the authors'
baseline conditions rather than transferring the competition recipes directly.
Establish sfGFP performance, preparation time, cost accounting, and variability
across independent preparation batches before introducing our own changes.

Then reproduce a published functional-group exchange, prioritizing the
energy-regeneration diagnostic. Record protein recovery and enzyme activity
where measurable, component-pool identity, template version, and purification
batch. Compare original and redesigned component-production templates using
otherwise matched assembly conditions, retaining the working preparation as a
control. This addresses the reported preparation failure without assuming that
its mechanism is the competition's limiting step.

A reconstituted model would require explicit component capacities and energy
chemistry beyond the current aggregate Machinery state. Measured component
activities could inform those capacities and prospective omission or titration
tests. The Popcorn interface could version component-production constructs
outside the competition's fixed-template scope. This track is a proposed
reproduction and model-development study; no OT-2 protocol has been implemented
or run by CFPS-Sim, and no collaboration with the POSTECH group is claimed.

\subsection{Data and decision infrastructure}
Curated chemical identities, reaction stoichiometry, contextual kinetic records,
and thermodynamic constraints should enter versioned evidence tables with
units, conditions, and source passages. A scientific-agent interface can help
extract and reconcile that evidence, while deterministic tools enforce
arithmetic and feasibility. Future evaluation should measure extraction error,
predictive error and interval coverage, and independently confirmed improvement
per total experimental budget. The decision to add detail should follow an
observable failure or a discriminating experiment, rather than an assumption
that a larger reaction network must select better recipes.

